mcqs:[q('Why did Theo score higher in the fictional study group?','He used retrieval practice that exposed gaps','He studied fewer hours on purpose','He highlighted more pages','He reread his highlights more','The type of effort mattered.'),q('Retrieval practice means:','Recalling information without looking, then checking','Rereading the text','Highlighting key passages','Listening to a summary','Recall strengthens memory.'),q('The illusion of fluency is:','Feeling you know material because it looks familiar','Speaking another language','Reading quickly','A type of grit','Familiarity is not the same as recall.'),q('Deliberate practice includes:','Focused work on weaknesses with feedback','Repeating what you already do well','Unlimited hours without goals','Avoiding feedback','It is targeted and effortful.'),q('How many questions per assigned book appear on the Medical Reading test?','10','5','25','50','The test has 50 questions, 10 per book.'),q('Which is a sign that persistence may be unproductive?','Repeating a strategy that practice shows is not working','Adjusting a plan after feedback','Taking breaks to rest','Asking for help','Healthy persistence adapts.'),q('A strong cross-book synthesis paragraph:','States a theme, cites two books accurately and notes a similarity and a difference','Forces every book to agree','Uses no page references','Summarizes one book only','Synthesis requires accurate comparison.'),q('Spaced practice means:','Spreading study sessions over time','Studying everything in one night','Studying in a large room','Leaving space in notes','Spacing improves long-term memory.')],
frq:{prompt:'Using the fictional study group, explain why Theo’s approach was more effective, distinguish productive persistence from unproductive persistence, and describe a study plan for the five-book Medical Reading event.',rubric:[['Explain retrieval and why it works',['retrieval','recall','gap','check']],['Distinguish productive and unproductive persistence',['strategy','burnout','adapt','rest','change']],['Describe a plan for 10 questions per book and the essay',['10','five books','spaced','interleav','essay','page']]],model:'Theo closed the book, wrote what he remembered and checked it, which is retrieval practice; it exposed gaps he could fix and strengthened memory, while Maya’s rereading created an illusion of fluency. Productive persistence directs effort at weaknesses and adapts when feedback shows a strategy is not working; unproductive persistence repeats ineffective methods or ignores exhaustion. My plan would be to read each of the five books fully with page-referenced notes, then use retrieval and spaced review, interleave books in later weeks, keep a list of names and details for the 10 questions per book, take timed mixed practice sets, write timed essay responses that synthesize two books, and protect sleep before the test.'},
